The Large Magellanic Cloud is approximately \(160{,}000\) light-years from Earth.

For \(v=0.99999987c\), the Lorentz factor is

\[
\gamma=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}}
=\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-0.99999987^2}}
\approx 1961.
\]

In the Earth–LMC frame, the one-way trip takes roughly \(160{,}000\) years. The astronaut experiences the proper time

\[
\Delta t_{\text{astronaut}}
=\frac{\Delta t_{\text{Earth}}}{\gamma}
\approx \frac{160{,}000}{1961}
\approx 82\text{ years},
\]

which is closest to the listed \(81\) years. The astronaut would arrive at age roughly \(22+82=104\) years, below the stated average lifetime of 150 years. This is a one-way trip, so the time should not be doubled.

Answer: A
